      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Reconstruction of moderate-sized mucosal defects of the oral cavity or oropharynx represents a surgical challenge. Buccinator myomucosal flaps seem to provide \"ideal reconstruction\" of oral/oropharyngeal defects because they carry a thin, mobile, well-vascularized, and sensitive tissue, like that excised or lost. Nevertheless, these flaps are not immediately popular because of confusion surrounding the complex terminology used to name them."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "After a retrospective study on our experience and a literature review, the authors propose a new rational and simplified nomenclature for the classification of buccinator myomucosal flaps, which clarifies the source vessel, the composition of the flap, and the type of transfer."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "According to this nomenclature, six types of buccinator myomucosal flaps are described."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This proposed nomenclature may bring a consensus on the classification of buccinator myomucosal flaps and can help their spread."
        }
      ]},
